rules.trigger:
`(?<![\p{L}\d.,])(\d{1,2})[ \t]?ft\.?[ \t]?(\d|1[01])(?!\d)`, then an optional `[ \t]?in\.?` that no letter follows. Only lower-case 'ft' and 'in' count.

When an 'in' follows the second number:
- glued to the number ('2in'): always inches;
- spaced, with a period ('4 in.'): inches;
- spaced, without a period: inches only before the end of the text, one of , . ; : ! ? ), or one of and, or, but, tall, high, long, wide, deep, thick, square, across. Before any other word it is a preposition and stays ('5 ft 6 in heels').

When no 'in' counts, the second number must be followed by the end of the text, one of , . ; : ! ? ), or a space and in, and, or, but, with, without, wearing or barefoot.

A number F, then a feet mark (', ’ or ′), an optional space, then inches M: one digit, or 10 or 11, with no leading zero and optional decimals. Then an optional inch mark (", ”, ″, '' or ’’).

The character before F must not be a letter, a digit, '.', ',', '°', ′, ', ’, ‘, ", “ or '#'. When there is an inch mark, no letter or digit may follow it.

When there is no inch mark:
- F must be a single digit from 1 to 9;
- M must be glued to the feet mark, or spaced only when the end of the text or one of , . ; : ! ? ) follows;
- M must not be followed by a letter, a digit, ' or ’;
- M must not be followed by -, –, : or / plus a digit.

rules.risks:
These are excluded:
- coordinates (a ° comes before, and 46 is more than 11);
- Swiss thousands such as 'CHF 1'250';
- durations such as 3'45" and 4'05";
- football minutes before a score ('Kane 9' 2-0');
- 'Type 6' 2 times'.

Still misread: a race split or an arc-minute under 12 written with marks ('4'10"', '5′10″') reads as a height. That is rare in general text.

rules.reads_as:
Each separator becomes ' by '.

It becomes ' times ' instead in any of these cases:
- '=', '≈', '^' or a superscript digit follows the last operand;
- '=' or a spaced + − - ÷ / comes right before the first operand;
- a spaced + − - ÷ / and a number come right after;
- 'equals' and a number follow;
- 'is', 'makes' or 'gives' follows, and the number after it equals the product of the operands ('3 x 3 is 9'; but '2x4 is 1.5 x 3.5' stays 'by');
- 'what is', 'what's', 'how much is', 'calculate' or 'compute' comes immediately before the first operand, with only spaces between. A match at the start of the clause is not enough: 'What is a 2x4?' and 'Calculate the area of a 10 x 12 room' are sizes. 'multiply' is dropped from this list, because 'multiply twelve by twelve' is natural English.

Inside a 'by' reading:
(a) Pixel sizes, meaning there is no unit other than px and every operand is a whole number of 100 or more. A three-digit operand not ending in 00 is emitted as a pair: '7 20' for 720, '2 50' for 250, '1 oh 5' for 105. Four-digit operands are already read as pairs by the number reader.
(b) An operand ending in exactly .5 with a whole part of 1 or more becomes 'N and a half' (pending the halves question).
(c) Units:
- When every unit is a mark of the same kind, the unit is read once, after the last number: '12' x 14'' becomes '12 by 14 feet', and '9" x 13" x 2" pan' becomes '9 by 13 by 2 inch pan'. This matches the merged inch rule.
- Written units are read where they are written: '35 cm x 48 cm' becomes '35 centimeters by 48 centimeters'.
- This pass writes the unit word itself (foot or feet, inch or inches, centimeter(s), millimeter(s), meter(s), yard(s), pixel(s)) instead of leaving it to the units rule, which is always plural. Singular or plural comes from the attributive test in the single-mark rule, applied to the whole expression: '20 x 30cm tin' gives 'centimeter tin', and '10' x 12' shed' gives 'foot shed'.

The unit is singular ('27 inch monitor', '6 foot fence') when any of these holds:
- N is 1;
- a determiner comes directly before the number (a, an, the, this, that, each, every, one, our, my, your, his, her, their, its), so 'a 6' high fence' gives '6 foot high fence';
- the word before the number is part of a product name: it contains a capital and isn't the first word of the sentence ('iMac 24"', 'MacBook Pro 16"');
- the next word is a noun, an adjective or a number word (in Swift, NLTagger's lexical class).

Otherwise it is plural: before punctuation or the end of the text, or before a function word ('27".', '8' high', '2" between', '2" off', '4" overnight', '6.1″ diagonally'). The fallback list of function words: prepositions, conjunctions, is/are/was/were, comparatives, -ly adverbs, and overnight, today, tonight, yesterday, tomorrow, ago, already, now, total, apart, away.

rules.trigger:
A whole or decimal number glued to x, X or ×, with no letter or digit after the multiplier. The number may follow a range dash ('2-3x', '5–10x'), but not a letter, digit, '.', ',', '_', '@', '#' or '/'. Then whitespace and one of:
- a comparative from a closed list: more, less, fewer, faster, slower, quicker, bigger, smaller, larger, cheaper, higher, lower, better, worse, longer, shorter, stronger, weaker, harder, easier, greater, lighter, heavier, wider, narrower, thinner, thicker, deeper, louder, quieter, brighter, safer, hotter, colder, warmer, cooler, denser, closer, further, farther, older, younger, richer, sooner, smarter, sharper, likelier, taller;
- 'as' plus a word from a closed list: much, many, fast, slow, quick, quickly, big, large, small, long, high, low, likely, good, bad, often, strong, heavy, expensive, cheap, far, hard, easy, bright, loud, efficient, powerful, effective. This replaces 'as plus anything but a determiner', which took 'as PNG' and 'as well';
- 'the' plus a quantity noun from a closed list: price, cost, size, amount, number, speed, rate, power, performance, value, capacity, memory, storage, weight, volume, damage, resolution, bandwidth, traffic, work, effort, money, time, length, distance, energy, output, revenue, risk. 'at 2x the whole time' stays;
- 'over', only when the end of the text or one of , . ; : ! ? ) follows ('3x over.'). 'at 2x over the speakers' stays;
- a frequency: 'a', 'an', 'per', 'each' or 'every' plus day, week, month, year, night, hour, session or game; or daily, weekly, monthly, yearly, annually, nightly or hourly; or 'in a row';
- a glued '/day', '/week', '/month', '/year', '/hour' or '/night'. Only the '3x' is rewritten; the slash is left to the slash-per rule.

These cases:
- a multiplier before a noun or on its own (2x speed, 3x zoom, 1.5x, 40x objective, 8x H100, 2x USB-C);
- clothing sizes (2X, 3XL);
- version wildcards (18.x, 2.x);
- the lexicon's '10x engineer';
- × glued to a number (10× faster, ×3);
- measurements already written as words (6-foot-2, five foot eleven);
- a British kiss sign-off ('See you at 8 x').

review_notes:
Rule bugs the stand-in found, each now guarded by a case:
- abbreviated rule: 'She's 5 ft 6 in heels' read 'five foot six heels';
- 'what is' / 'calculate' at the start of a clause: 'What is a 2x4?' and 'Calculate the area of a 10 x 12 room' read 'times';
- 'is' plus a number: 'A 2x4 is 1.5 x 3.5 inches' read 'two times four';
- 'a spaced + and a number follow': 'Factor 3x2 + 5x + 2' read 'three times two';
- the attributive test: 'Build a 6' high fence' read 'six feet high fence', 'Trim 2" off' and 'We got 4" overnight' read 'inch', and 'The iMac 24" comes' read 'inches';
- 'as' plus a word: 'at 2x as PNG' and '2x as well' read 'times';
- 'the': 'at 2x the whole time' read 'times';
- 'over': 'at 2x over the speakers' read 'times';
- dimensions read 'by' in '3 x 12 hour shifts', '2 x 20cm round cake tins', '2 x 4 packs of batteries' and 'Brazil won 2 x 1';
- single ' foot mark: 'She called it 'Plan 9' long before' read 'nine feet long';
- game rule: 'Now solve for x2!' read 'times two';
- marked heights with no inch mark: a spaced form ('Type 6' 2 times') is now allowed only before punctuation.
